The new fixture entry `{ id: 'cond_ado', prompt: './agents/cond_ado.md', condition: 'c.js' }` (`cond_ado.md` deliberately missing from the virtual FS) exercises the new `!extracted.conditional` skip — but nothing asserts it. The only check on this result is `assert.ok(rEdge.errors.length >= 6)` (test_agentValidator.js:381): if the conditional skip regressed (e.g. the `!extracted.conditional` guard at `js/agentValidator.js:162` was dropped), errors would go 7 → 8 and the bound still passes. The strengthened per-agent test doesn't cover this either — all conditional prompt files in the real agent JSONs exist, so a regression there produces no errors.

Net effect: a future refactor that re-enables file-existence checks for conditional tracker entries would ship silently and start failing `validateAll` for legitimate configs whose conditional prompts are generated at runtime (the dev-leg plan-appendix pattern — `js/preCliDevelopmentSetup.js:1027`).

Suggested fix — make one assertion sensitive to the entry, e.g.:

```js
assert.deepEqual(rEdge.errors.filter(function(e) {
    return e.indexOf('cond_ado') !== -1;
}), [], 'conditional tracker entries must not require the prompt file to exist');
```

(or pin the exact expected error count instead of the `>= 6` bound). This mirrors the fixture intent already present for the `cliPrompts` path and keeps the new behavior regression-safe.

